# =============================================================================
# nosend.dev — Cloudflare Pages Redirects
# =============================================================================
#
# File:     public/_redirects
# Deploy:   Cloudflare Pages reads this file automatically on every build
# Docs:     https://developers.cloudflare.com/pages/configuration/redirects/
#
# PURPOSE
# -------
# This file handles three categories of redirect:
#
#   1. CANONICALIZATION — collapse duplicate URLs to a single canonical form.
#      Example: /index.html → /  (same content, one URL for SEO).
#
#   2. CONVENIENCE — short, memorable aliases for commonly-typed URLs.
#      Example: /privacy → /privacy.html  (no extension to remember).
#
#   3. FUTURE-PROOFING — 301 permanent redirects from old slugs to new slugs.
#      Example: /tools/csv-merge → /tools/csv-merger/ (if slug is ever renamed).
#
# Every rule in this file uses 301 (permanent). This is deliberate:
#   • 301 transfers ~99% of SEO link equity from old URL to new URL.
#   • Browsers and CDNs cache 301s long-term — good for performance.
#   • These URLs are permanent decisions, not A/B tests. Use 302 only for
#     temporary experiments — never for a URL you plan to keep.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES *NOT* DO (and why)
# --------------------------------------
#   • No SPA fallback. This is a STATIC site — every URL maps to a real
#     file. A catch-all `/*  /index.html  200` rule would return HTTP 200
#     for every non-existent page, hiding 404s from Google (major SEO bug)
#     and confusing users. Every URL must resolve to a real file or 404.
#
#   • No /404.html redirect. Cloudflare Pages ALREADY serves public/404.html
#     automatically with HTTP status 404 for unknown routes. Redirecting
#     unknown paths to /404.html (via 301/302) would change the status code
#     to 3xx, causing crawlers to index the error page as a valid URL —
#     a textbook SEO anti-pattern. Leave Cloudflare's 404 handling alone.
#
#   • No www ↔ apex rule. Redirecting www.nosend.dev ↔ nosend.dev is
#     handled at the Cloudflare DNS / Redirect Rules layer, BEFORE requests
#     reach the Pages project. A `_redirects` rule here would be dead code
#     (never evaluated for cross-origin www traffic).
#
#   • No trailing-slash 308 overrides. When a directory contains index.html,
#     Cloudflare automatically sends a 308 from /tools/x → /tools/x/. Adding
#     explicit rules for this would either conflict or create a redirect
#     loop. Trust the platform; do not "fix" what is not broken.
#
#   • No asset redirects. /assets/* paths are served directly as files.
#     Redirecting them would break Cache-Control headers (see _headers),
#     Content-Security-Policy allowlists, and any future Subresource
#     Integrity (SRI) hashes. Never add /assets/* rules here.
#
#   • No api.nosend.dev rules. api.nosend.dev is a Cloudflare Worker with
#     its own routing. Requests to api.* never reach Pages, so any rule
#     here would be unreachable dead code. The Worker is configured in
#     worker/wrangler.toml.
#
#   • No external redirects. No rule in this file may redirect to a domain
#     other than nosend.dev. External redirects are an open-redirect
#     vulnerability — a phishing vector and a Google ranking penalty.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SYNTAX (Cloudflare Pages spec)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   [source]     [destination]     [status-code]
#
#   • Source MUST start with "/" (root-relative path).
#   • Destination MUST start with "/" (internal) or "https://nosend.dev/"
#     (same-origin absolute). Never a different domain.
#   • Status: 200 (rewrite), 301, 302, 303, 307, 308.
#     200 = proxy (URL bar unchanged, content from elsewhere) — DO NOT USE.
#     301 = permanent (SEO transfer, cached long-term) — DEFAULT HERE.
#     302 = temporary (no SEO transfer) — only for A/B tests.
#   • Placeholders:  /:slug    — matches exactly ONE path segment.
#   • Splats:        /path/*   — matches EVERYTHING after /path/ (one max).
#   • Comments start with "#" on their own line.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RULE PRECEDENCE — CRITICAL (opposite of _headers)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   • In `_headers`: LAST matching rule wins (most specific goes at bottom).
#   • In `_redirects`: FIRST matching rule wins (most specific goes at top).
#
#   This is because redirects represent URL transitions — once you've matched
#   the right source pattern, further rules would create a chain. Order this
#   file: specific patterns BEFORE general patterns.
#
#   Hard limit: 2,000 static + 100 dynamic rules per Cloudflare Pages project.
#   We currently use ~20. Headroom is enormous.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PATH CONVENTION — INTENTIONAL EXCEPTION TO PROJECT RULE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The project-wide rule says: "No leading slash in local file references."
# (e.g., href="assets/css/style.css", not "/assets/css/style.css")
#
# This file is the ONE EXCEPTION. Every source and destination in `_redirects`
# MUST start with "/". Why:
#
#   • HTML relative paths resolve against the CURRENT PAGE URL — they must
#     work under file://, localhost, and any deployment subdirectory. So
#     "assets/css/style.css" → "../../assets/css/style.css" depending on
#     page depth.
#
#   • `_redirects` source patterns are matched against the incoming HTTP
#     REQUEST PATH at Cloudflare's edge — there is no "current page".
#     They must be absolute. A source of "privacy" (no slash) is a syntax
#     error and the entire rule is silently ignored.
#
# Do NOT "fix" the leading slashes below. They are correct and required.
#
# Last updated: 2026-10-23
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1. HOMEPAGE CANONICALIZATION
# =============================================================================
# The homepage is served at "/" (Cloudflare resolves "/" → /index.html).
# A direct request to /index.html returns the SAME content under a DIFFERENT
# URL — classic duplicate content. Google indexes both, splits page rank,
# and may pick the wrong one as canonical. This rule fixes that.
#
# /index (no .html) catches users who type the URL by hand.
#
/index.html    /    301
/index         /    301


# =============================================================================
# 2. LEGAL PAGE ALIASES
# =============================================================================
# Actual files: privacy.html, terms.html. These aliases let users type
# /privacy or /terms without the extension — cleaner for business cards,
# printed materials, and verbal references ("nosend.dev/privacy").
#
# The real URLs still work: /privacy.html and /terms.html resolve directly
# to their files with NO redirect (Cloudflare serves the file). Only the
# extensionless form redirects. No redirect chain, no double-hop.
#
/privacy       /privacy.html    301
/terms         /terms.html      301


# =============================================================================
# 3. PRICING PAGE ALIAS
# =============================================================================
# Same rationale as legal pages. /pricing is cleaner in ad copy, tweets,
# and HN comments where every character counts.
#
/pricing       /pricing.html    301


# =============================================================================
# 4. TOOL INDEX.HTML CANONICALIZATION
# =============================================================================
# Each tool lives at public/tools/<slug>/index.html. Cloudflare serves it
# when you request /tools/<slug>/ — but /tools/<slug>/index.html ALSO works,
# creating a duplicate URL. This rule collapses both to the canonical form.
#
# :slug matches EXACTLY ONE path segment. Nested paths like
# /tools/x/y/index.html (which would be a mistake) are NOT captured — they
# 404 naturally, which is the correct behavior.
#
# The trailing slash in the destination is REQUIRED — Cloudflare's automatic
# 308 for /tools/<slug> → /tools/<slug>/ means that if we redirected to
# /tools/<slug> (no slash), the user would receive TWO redirects in series
# (ours + Cloudflare's). One redirect is faster and cleaner.
#
/tools/:slug/index.html    /tools/:slug/    301


# =============================================================================
# 5. TOOL SHORT-LINK ALIASES
# =============================================================================
# Marketing-friendly short URLs. Useful for:
#   • Social posts (Twitter's 280-char limit, LinkedIn captions)
#   • Product Hunt / Hacker News launch text
#   • QR codes and print materials
#   • Verbal references ("try nosend.dev/pdf")
#
# Every destination uses the canonical trailing-slash form — see section 4
# for the rationale. Avoids a redirect chain.
#
# NOTE: During pre-launch development, some tool directories may not exist
# yet. The redirect rules still fire (they're evaluated before file lookup),
# but they would land on a 404 until the tool ships. This is expected and
# correct — the URL contract is defined NOW, the destination fills in later.
#
/csv                 /tools/csv-merger/          301
/csv-merger          /tools/csv-merger/          301
/csv-to-json         /tools/csv-to-json/         301

/json                /tools/json-formatter/      301
/json-formatter      /tools/json-formatter/      301

/image-compress      /tools/image-compressor/    301
/image-convert       /tools/image-converter/     301

/pdf                 /tools/pdf-merger/          301
/pdf-merger          /tools/pdf-merger/          301


# =============================================================================
# 6. TOOL SLUG FIXUPS (FUTURE-PROOFING)
# =============================================================================
# If a tool slug is ever renamed (e.g., csv-merge → csv-merger during beta),
# add a 301 here so old inbound links and Google's index transfer cleanly.
#
# These are ANTICIPATED aliases — variants of the current slugs that were
# considered during planning but did not become canonical. They are safe
# to keep: the destination pages exist, so there is no risk of 404s for
# legitimate traffic. They also pre-empt common typos.
#
# When you rename a tool in the future:
#   1. Move public/tools/OLD/ → public/tools/NEW/
#   2. Add a rule below: /tools/OLD/  /tools/NEW/  301
#   3. Update sitemap.xml and internal links
#   4. Keep this rule FOREVER (301s are permanent; removal causes 404s)
#
/tools/csv-merge          /tools/csv-merger/        301
/tools/image-compress     /tools/image-compressor/  301
/tools/json-format        /tools/json-formatter/    301
/tools/image-convert      /tools/image-converter/   301


# =============================================================================
# DELIBERATELY OMITTED — DO NOT ADD THESE
# =============================================================================
#
# The following rules look useful but are actively harmful. Each is listed
# with the specific reason it must never appear in this file.
#
# ❌  /*                    /index.html    200
#     SPA fallback. Would return HTTP 200 for every unknown URL, hiding all
#     404s from Google. Devastating for SEO (soft-404 penalty). Every tool
#     is a real HTML file — there is no router, no need for a fallback.
#
# ❌  /*                    /404.html      404
#     `_redirects` does not support 404 status. Status codes allowed:
#     200, 301, 302, 303, 307, 308. Attempting 404 here breaks the rule.
#     Cloudflare already serves 404.html with proper 404 status automatically.
#
# ❌  /old-page             https://external.com/page    301
#     Open redirect. Google penalizes it. Phishers abuse it. NO external
#     destinations, ever. If you migrate to a different domain, do it at
#     the Cloudflare DNS layer with a Bulk Redirect List — not in _redirects.
#
# ❌  /assets/*             /somewhere-else    301
#     Would break Cache-Control (from _headers), Content-Security-Policy
#     allowlists, and any future SRI hashes. Assets are static files — never
#     redirect them.
#
# ❌  /tools/csv-merger     /tools/csv-merger/  301
#     Redundant. Cloudflare Pages already sends an automatic 308 for this
#     pattern (because the directory contains index.html). Adding our own
#     301 would run BEFORE Cloudflare's automatic rule, creating a 301→308
#     chain. Always trust Cloudflare's automatic handling.
#
# ❌  /                      /home             301
#     Creates a redirect from the homepage. Google treats "/" as the site
#     root. Redirecting "/" breaks that assumption, triggers a "redirect
#     on homepage" warning in Search Console, and confuses users. Never
#     redirect "/" anywhere.
#
# ❌  /privacy.html          /privacy          301
#     Redirecting FROM the real file TO the alias is backwards. The real
#     file is canonical; the alias is a convenience. Our rule goes
#     /privacy → /privacy.html (correct direction), never the reverse.
#
# ❌  /ANYTHING              /ANYTHING         301
#     Self-redirect. Causes an infinite redirect loop. Cloudflare rejects
#     these at deploy time, but you may see build errors without a clear
#     message. Always verify destination ≠ source (modulo query strings).


# =============================================================================
# HOW TO VERIFY DEPLOYMENT
# =============================================================================
#
# After `wrangler pages deploy public` (or a GitHub-triggered build), run
# these commands to verify each section of this file works as intended.
#
# Use `curl -sI` to fetch headers only (fast) and `curl -sIL` to follow
# redirects and see the full chain (detects loops and chains).
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Homepage canonicalization
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/index.html | grep -iE '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301
#     location: /
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Extensionless legal pages
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/privacy | grep -iE '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301
#     location: /privacy.html
#
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/terms | grep -iE '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301
#     location: /terms.html
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Pricing page
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/pricing | grep -iE '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301
#     location: /pricing.html
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Tool index.html canonicalization
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/tools/csv-merger/index.html | grep -iE '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301
#     location: /tools/csv-merger/
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Tool short links
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/pdf | grep -iE '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301
#     location: /tools/pdf-merger/
#
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/csv | grep -iE '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301
#     location: /tools/csv-merger/
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Slug fixups
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/tools/csv-merge | grep -iE '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301
#     location: /tools/csv-merger/
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. 404 behavior — MUST be HTTP 404, NOT a redirect
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sI https://nosend.dev/this-does-not-exist | head -n1
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 404
#
#   If you see 301/302 instead, a forbidden catch-all rule exists somewhere
#   in this file. Remove it.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Redirect chain detection (each redirect should resolve in ONE hop)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sIL https://nosend.dev/tools/csv-merge | grep -E '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 301          ← /tools/csv-merge  (from our rule)
#     location: /tools/csv-merger/
#     HTTP/2 200          ← final destination (served from file)
#
#   If you see THREE or more HTTP lines, there is a redirect chain.
#   Common cause: destination missing trailing slash → Cloudflare's
#   automatic 308 → 301 + 308 chain. Fix: add trailing slash to destination.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Redirect loop detection
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   curl -sIL https://nosend.dev/ | grep -E '^(HTTP|location)'
#   Expected:
#     HTTP/2 200          ← single response, no redirect
#
#   Any `location:` line pointing back to "/" indicates a self-redirect
#   somewhere in the file. Cloudflare usually rejects these at deploy time,
#   but always verify after editing.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# If any of these checks fail, diagnose in this order:
#   1. Case sensitivity — paths are case-sensitive. /PRIVACY ≠ /privacy.
#      (Our rules use lowercase — match the actual file names.)
#   2. Rule order — first match wins. Verify with `curl -v` tracing.
#   3. Deploy succeeded — Cloudflare Pages → Deployments → latest build.
#   4. CDN cache — Cloudflare caches 301s for 24h. Purge after fixing rules:
#      Cloudflare Dashboard → Caching → Purge Everything (or by URL).
#   5. Browser cache — hard-refresh (Cmd+Shift+R / Ctrl+F5) after CDN purge.


# =============================================================================
# WHEN TO UPDATE THIS FILE
# =============================================================================
#
# ADD a rule when:
#   • A tool slug is renamed                 → 301 old → new (keep forever)
#   • A page URL changes                     → 301 old → new (keep forever)
#   • A marketing short-link is desired      → e.g., /excel, /word
#   • A common typo needs handling           → e.g., /pricng → /pricing
#   • A legacy URL from a domain migration   → 301 old → new
#
# NEVER add a rule that:
#   • Redirects to a domain other than nosend.dev
#   • Returns 200 for a missing page (SPA fallback)
#   • Redirects to /404.html (breaks proper 404 status)
#   • Applies to /assets/* or /.well-known/* paths
#   • Creates a chain of two or more redirects (curl-test the full path)
#   • Redirects FROM "/" anywhere (Google penalizes)
#   • Redirects TO itself (infinite loop)
#
# When in doubt: do nothing. A 404 served with public/404.html (which has a
# beautiful "Browse all tools" UI) is almost always better UX than a
# confusing redirect. Only redirect when the intent is genuinely unambiguous.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Redirects are like API contracts — once published, assume they will be
# requested forever. Google's index, user bookmarks, forum posts, and
# social media links all point to these URLs. If you add a rule, plan to
# keep it running for the lifetime of the domain.
